From: Petr Wozniak <petr.wozniak@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] map-agent 99994: report a client from the link it is held under

The periodic per-STA refresh fetches the whole MLO station and then uses
mlsta.sta[0] for the metrics. Since libwifi 940 every affiliated link carries
its own rssi, so reading link 0 alone reports whichever link happens to come
first - and on an idle link that is an honest zero, which rssi_to_rcpi() turns
into 255.

Measured on hardware 2026-08-03, iPhone associated with three links on x8:

    kernel                            libwifi (ubus)
    link 0:  0 [0, 0] dBm             link_id 0  rssi_avg 0
    link 1:  0 [0, 0, 0] dBm          link_id 1  rssi_avg 0
    link 2: -46 [-71, -46, -53] dBm   link_id 2  rssi_avg -45

The agent reported rssi 0 for all three link addresses and mapc.db held
rcpi 255, so every rcpi-based decision - steering thresholds, hysteresis,
reporting - was made on a number that described nothing. The data was two
layers below the whole time.

An MLO client sits in ap->stalist under the address of the link it associated
on, so that link is the one this BSS is entitled to report. Prefer it. If it
has no measurement, because the client is parked elsewhere and this link is
idle, fall back to the strongest link that does have one: a client heard at -45
on some link is not a client at the noise floor. Zero survives only when no
link measured anything.

Deliberately left alone: stamld_update() above still keys off sta[0]. It records
which link the MLD is on rather than how well it is heard, and changing it is a
separate question from the metrics.

Not addressed here: the kernel still reports a last-ack RSSI on links whose
data-frame signal is zero (-14 and -13 dBm on the two idle links above), which
is a better source for a link the client is not currently transmitting on.
libwifi does not surface it yet.
--- a/src/agent.c
+++ b/src/agent.c
@@ -1527,7 +1527,54 @@
 			stamld_update(a, ap, mlsta.macaddr, mlsta.bssid,
 				      mlsta.sta[0].mlo_link_id, s);
 
-		update_sta_entry(a, ap, &(mlsta.sta[0]));
+		/* Take the metrics from the link this client is actually held
+		 * under here, not from link 0.
+		 *
+		 * wifi_get_mld_station() returns every affiliated link, and since
+		 * libwifi 940 each one carries its own rssi. Reading sta[0] alone
+		 * reports whichever link happens to be first, which for an idle
+		 * link is an honest zero - and rssi_to_rcpi(0) is 255. Measured on
+		 * hardware 2026-08-03, an iPhone with three links on x8:
+		 *
+		 *     link 0  rssi_avg 0     (idle)
+		 *     link 1  rssi_avg 0     (idle)
+		 *     link 2  rssi_avg -45   (carrying the traffic)
+		 *
+		 * The agent reported 0 for all three and the controller stored 255,
+		 * so every rcpi-based decision was made on a number that described
+		 * nothing.
+		 *
+		 * An MLO client sits in ap->stalist under the address of the link it
+		 * associated on, so that link is the one this BSS is entitled to
+		 * report. If it has no measurement - the client is parked on another
+		 * link and this one is idle - fall back to the strongest link that
+		 * does, because a client heard at -45 somewhere is not a client at
+		 * the noise floor. Zero stays zero only when no link measured
+		 * anything.
+		 */
+		{
+			struct wifi_sta *e = &mlsta.sta[0];
+			int li;
+
+			for (li = 0; li < mlsta.num_link; li++) {
+				if (!memcmp(mlsta.sta[li].macaddr, s->macaddr, 6)) {
+					e = &mlsta.sta[li];
+					break;
+				}
+			}
+
+			if (e->rssi_avg == 0) {
+				for (li = 0; li < mlsta.num_link; li++) {
+					if (mlsta.sta[li].rssi_avg == 0)
+						continue;
+					if (e->rssi_avg == 0 ||
+					    mlsta.sta[li].rssi_avg > e->rssi_avg)
+						e = &mlsta.sta[li];
+				}
+			}
+
+			update_sta_entry(a, ap, e);
+		}
 	}
 #endif
 
